{
  "method": "Two separate processes, non-interleaved. Original before DLL and root-built after DLL were copied to separate /tmp projects; neither probe references repository projects or uses shared build output. Same runtime, filesystem root, prepared-candidate counts, 3-call warmup and operation order. Each process creates its own fresh initialized SQLite fixture with newly generated IDs/paths of equivalent shape.",
  "interpretation": "The raw field uninterruptedCallerOccupationMs is a sum of invocation durations. Before samples completed 100/100 synchronously with all continuations on the initial thread, establishing continuous caller occupation for the 100-call loops. After sums are aggregate invocation costs across released continuations, not a continuous UI stall duration. No Windows dispatcher, UI frame, source inspection, actual file copy or native rendering was measured.",
  "limitations": "One representative pass each, not an interleaved throughput benchmark. Batch time/CPU differences include OS scheduling, provider warmup and measurement overhead; no SQLite throughput improvement or CPU reduction is claimed. Small operations can finish before the caller observes the returned task; zero synchronous completions is observed for this pass, not a contract.",
  "compatibilityCost": "Owned-file import already executes on a worker; the shared boundary adds a scheduling hop. The owned 10-row sample batch rose from 8.4051 to 9.2672 ms and CPU from 8.434 to 9.809 ms. Duplicate-return batch rose from 19.174 to 23.5423 ms. Production clipboard uses its existing worker and AddClipboardFilesAsync; the single-file API sample verifies a supported compatibility path only and is not a clipboard UI benefit claim.",
